+/**
+ * Default backlog, in queued frames of video, that clients are allowed to fall
+ * behind by before acknowledgement of further graphics frames is held back.
+ * Overridden by the GUAC_RDP_H264_BACKLOG_TARGET environment variable, where
+ * zero disables holding entirely.
+ *
+ * This is a backstop, not a rate controller, and the distinction is what sets
+ * the value. A client normally runs a frame or two behind, and rises and falls
+ * around that as decode cost varies with scene complexity. A target inside
+ * that working range is tripped continuously: the queue fills, the ack is
+ * held, the queue drains, and it fills again, delivering frames in bursts
+ * separated by stalls -- measurably worse than not throttling at all, because
+ * the stalls are visible and the backlog was never dangerous.
+ *
+ * The value must therefore sit above the depth a healthy session reaches and
+ * below the 120-frame cap in guac_display_layer_set_h264(), where the oldest
+ * frames are dropped and the decoder's reference chain breaks. 32 leaves room
+ * for ordinary jitter while still engaging well before that cliff.
+ */
+#define GUAC_RDP_H264_BACKLOG_TARGET_DEFAULT 32
+
+/**
+ * Default client processing lag, in milliseconds, that is tolerated before
+ * acknowledgement of further graphics frames is held back. Overridden by the
+ * GUAC_RDP_H264_LAG_TARGET environment variable, where zero disables the lag
+ * gate and leaves only the backlog gate.
+ *
+ * This is the primary gate, and the backlog is a backstop behind it. The
+ * backlog only measures frames still held by guacd; once a frame has been
+ * flushed to the socket it is invisible there, even though the client may not
+ * have decoded or drawn it yet. On a fast link the queue therefore drains to
+ * nothing while the client falls further and further behind, which is exactly
+ * the case an acknowledgement is supposed to report upstream.
+ *
+ * guac_client_get_processing_lag() measures the round trip of the Guacamole
+ * sync handshake, which terminates at the client after it has processed the
+ * frame, and subtracts its estimate of network round-trip time
+ * (user-handlers.c). What remains is the client's own inability to keep up --
+ * decode cost, compositing, an AVC444 chroma combine -- none of which the RDP
+ * server can observe on its own. Because the network component is already
+ * removed, an absolute threshold is meaningful on a WAN as well as a LAN.
+ *
+ * The render thread waits out this same signal before sending the next frame
+ * (display-render-thread.c), so gating acknowledgement on it makes the
+ * upstream and downstream halves of the pipeline agree rather than leaving
+ * guacd to absorb the difference in its queue until frames are dropped.
+ */
+#define GUAC_RDP_H264_LAG_TARGET_DEFAULT 50
+
+/**
+ * The longest a single graphics frame acknowledgement may be held back, in
+ * milliseconds. Overridden by the GUAC_RDP_H264_ACK_MAX_DELAY environment
+ * variable.
+ *
